<node id="&quot;ISSUING LENDER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LENDER"</data>
  <data key="d1">The "Issuing Lender" is defined as a financial institution authorized to issue letters of credit and provide funds for disbursements in accordance with customary international banking practices. This designation encompasses specific entities like Citibank, N.A., which participates in the credit agreement by issuing letters of credit and managing associated payment responsibilities. The Issuing Lender plays a crucial role in a lending arrangement, as it is responsible for documentation, compliance, and financial transactions involving the Borrower and Administrative Agent.

Further, the Issuing Lender guarantees payment under letters of credit and ensures that the conditions for these instruments are met as per the agreement. Each Issuing Lender agrees to issue standby Letters of Credit, maintain accounts related to credits made to the Borrower, and monitor its commitments under the loan document. They also have obligations to approve or deny requests related to letters of credit, and they hold rights to reimbursement from the Borrower for any amounts paid under these agreements.

Within the lending structure, the Issuing Lender collaborates with other lenders and their administrative agents to facilitate loan provisions, including issuing financial instruments and ensuring the availability of funds for payment obligations. The obligations and rights of the Issuing Lender are specifically defined in the agreement, emphasizing their role as essential players in the financing process and their responsibilities in guaranteeing payments. The Issuing Lender must uphold regulatory and legal standards while managing potential risks linked to the letters of credit they issue. Overall, the Issuing Lender is a key component of the arrangement, directly influencing the financial interactions and obligations between the party that borrows and those providing funds.</data>
